00013 successfully fixes the previous store-engine issues. I checked the report and the actual updated PR #3. GitHub shows it still open, unmerged, and mergeable, now at head b52cca8f7ca9d1017b53e97cb6c46091792f5070.

The continuity lock, complete-block requirement, immutable child-set checks, orphan re-promotion, and concurrency hardening all look sound. In particular, sync_state ... FOR UPDATE now really does serialize canonical mutations, and the old-orphan corruption path we found is gone.

I would still not merge yet, because this pass against Qogecoin Core itself exposed two important UTXO-semantic mismatches that our synthetic store tests never exercised, plus one transaction-shape hole.

1. Genesis coinbase must not become a UTXO

This is a definite Core mismatch.

Qogecoin Core explicitly special-cases the genesis block in ConnectBlock() and skips connecting its transactions because the genesis coinbase is unspendable. Chainparams says the same thing: the genesis transaction output cannot be spent because it was never inserted into the coins database.

The current explorer does the opposite. applyOutput() unconditionally creates a utxo_state row for every transaction output.

That means when we finally index QOGE from height 0, the explorer would incorrectly create a spendable-looking UTXO for the genesis transaction:

a0bc982915c0435f85fa6e44b7e6bd7b32e2a6ad10f968d223d4a56fa2aabc9e

and potentially show its 100 QOGE genesis output as an address balance even though Core has no such UTXO.

The immutable transaction_outputs row should absolutely remain. Only canonical utxo_state creation must be skipped for genesis.

2. OP_RETURN and other provably-unspendable outputs must not enter utxo_state

This is another direct Core mismatch.

Qogecoin Core's CCoinsViewCache::AddCoin() immediately returns without creating a coin when the output script is IsUnspendable(). Core defines that precisely as a script that starts with OP_RETURN, or a script larger than MAX_SCRIPT_SIZE (10,000 bytes).

Our explorer currently preserves the OP_RETURN output—which is correct—but also creates an unspent utxo_state row for it.

So our test requirement “preserve zero-value OP_RETURN” was only half complete. We need:

transaction_outputs:
    YES — preserve it forever

utxo_state:
    NO — Core never added it to the UTXO set

That distinction is exactly why separating immutable outputs from canonical UTXO state was useful.

3. IsCoinbase can currently contradict the actual input structure

Core defines coinbase structurally as:

vin.size() == 1
AND
vin[0].prevout.IsNull()

Our Go model represents the null prevout as PreviousOut == nil, but validateBlockShape() currently checks only TxCount and positional vin/vout indexes.

Consequently a malformed canonical model can currently say:

IsCoinbase = true

Inputs:
    PreviousOut = some real tx:vout

and Store will treat it as coinbase: no fee computation and, worse, no prevout spend marking. Its outputs would still become UTXOs. That's an easy route for an RPC-decoder bug to inflate explorer balances even though PostgreSQL remains internally consistent.

Since IsCoinbase directly controls monetary accounting, Store should enforce its structural equivalence before any writes. At block level it is also cheap to require transaction 0 to be the sole coinbase.

There is one smaller multisig edge I would clean up in the same pass: output_participants is keyed by address, but replay completeness currently compares its row count with len(PubKeys). A bare multisig script containing the same pubkey twice would collapse to one participant identity on first insert and then fail its own exact replay. Raw script already preserves the duplicate, so output_participants should explicitly have set-of-identities semantics and deduplicate identical (address,pubkey) participants.

